// A cross-package facade that publishes its one export at the end of a
// FOUR-hop local alias chain, over a require that crosses a package
// boundary into one of two indistinguishable installs.
//
// Four separate wrong answers are available here, and every one of them
// reads as "resolved" rather than as an error:
//
//  1. TRUNCATION -- stop after one alias hop and the export has no value
//     at all, which is UNKNOWN: safe, but the whole point of following the
//     chain is that this shape is resolvable and this answer is not the
//     truth.
//  2. NAME FALLBACK -- give up on the chain and search this file for a
//     function named after the export. `dangerousOp` below is exactly that
//     decoy: a private helper this package never publishes and never
//     calls, whose only relationship to the export is its spelling
//     (RWF-011). It is declared BEFORE the chain, so "first match in the
//     file" finds it.
//  3. WRONG INSTANCE -- resolve the chain, then attribute it to
//     node_modules/vt2-vuln-lib. That twin has the same name, the same
//     version and the same export name as the install this file's require
//     really reaches, so any resolution keyed on package metadata rather
//     than on path lands there.
//  4. WRONG PACKAGE -- attribute the target to vt2-chain-lib, where the
//     export is physically spelled. Superficially defensible, completely
//     wrong.
//
// Node's answer is none of those: `require("vt2-vuln-lib")` from THIS file
// resolves to this package's own nested install, because a nested
// node_modules is consulted before its parents'. Every hop below is a
// module-scope binding this file declares exactly once and never writes to
// again, so the chain is a proof rather than a guess.
function dangerousOp(input) {
  return "chain-local-helper:" + input;
}

const dep = require("vt2-vuln-lib");
const hopOne = dep;
const hopTwo = hopOne;
const impl = hopTwo.dangerousOp;
const published = impl;

exports.localHelper = dangerousOp;
exports.dangerousOp = published;
